% Checking-unit numbering for function equations.
%
% Each equation is its own checking unit, and a unit that reports an
% error omits its warnings. Three things have to hold at once for the
% right warnings to survive, and this program pins all three:
%
%   * equation units continue from the rules, one per equation of a
%     *plain* function;
%   * a class function's equations consume none, because they are
%     checked in throwaway sub-sessions whose diagnostics are dropped;
%   * the walker's numbering agrees, equation for equation, with the
%     constructor-arity walk's — an arity error and the solver errors
%     of the same equation must land in one unit.
%
% `cf` is the class function whose equations must not consume a unit.
% `f`'s second equation is dead but otherwise fine, so its warning
% survives. `g`'s first equation is dead *and* wrong, so its warning is
% suppressed by its own error. `k`'s only equation is dead *and* uses a
% constructor at the wrong arity, so its warning is suppressed by an
% error that comes from the arity walk rather than from the solver —
% which is the case that pins the two numberings against each other.
:- module(typecheck_fun_equation_units, [go/1]).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

:- chr_constraint go(int).
:- chr_type box ---> b(int).

:- class (cf(int) -> int), (cf(string) -> string).
cf(N) -> N.

:- function (f(int) -> int).
f(1) -> 1.
f("oops") -> 1.

:- function (g(int) -> int).
g("x") -> "y".
g(N) -> N.

:- function (k(int) -> int).
k("z") -> b(1, 2).

go(R) <=> R is f(1) + g(1) + k(1).
